{
  "image_name": "431c83306aa1e02f9487b42295acf142.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three synthetic routes for making branched or star-shaped “miktoarm” polymers from a branched core bearing orthogonal reactive groups, and shows how these architectures lead to self-assembled nanostructures with useful functions.\n\nOverall, the left side emphasizes drug-delivery micelles. In one route, ring-opening polymerization, ester-based drug conjugation, and a click reaction produce an amphiphilic miktoarm polymer with a linked drug; this then self-assembles and can also encapsulate additional cargo, giving micellar nanocarriers that contain both conjugated and physically trapped drugs. A second route combines atom transfer radical polymerization, click chemistry, and radical addition–fragmentation chain transfer polymerization to create a miktoarm polymer with arms of predetermined functions. These molecules self-assemble into dual-responsive micelles, depicted with a photoresponsive core and a thermoresponsive corona.\n\nThe right side shows a one-pot copper-mediated reversible deactivation radical polymerization approach using monomers such as N-isopropylacrylamide and 2-hydroxyethyl acrylate plus a crosslinker. This yields a core-crosslinked star miktoarm polymer, which further self-assembles into higher-order thermoresponsive organic nanoparticles.\n\nTaken together, the figure highlights how controlled polymer chemistry and arm functionality determine micelle structure, responsiveness, and drug-loading behavior.",
